## Solution

The correct statement is **(a) R is a field**.

### Proof

Let $R$ be an integral domain, so $R[x]$ is also an integral domain and $(0)\subset R[x]$ is a prime ideal. The hypothesis says: every **non-zero** prime ideal of $R[x]$ is maximal. Hence any strict chain of prime ideals in $R[x]$ has the form
$$(0)\subsetneq \mathfrak{p},\qquad \mathfrak{p}\text{ maximal},$$
so $\dim R[x]\le 1$.

**Key inequality (general, no Noetherian hypothesis).** For *any* ring $R$,
$$\dim R[x]\ge \dim R+1.$$
Indeed, given a chain of prime ideals $\mathfrak{p}_0\subsetneq\mathfrak{p}_1\subsetneq\cdots\subsetneq\mathfrak{p}_n$ in $R$, the extended ideals give
$$\mathfrak{p}_0[x]\subsetneq\mathfrak{p}_1[x]\subsetneq\cdots\subsetneq\mathfrak{p}_n[x]\subsetneq(\mathfrak{p}_n,x).$$
Each $\mathfrak{p}_i[x]$ is prime in $R[x]$, and $(\mathfrak{p}_n,x)$ is prime because $R[x]/(\mathfrak{p}_n,x)\cong R/\mathfrak{p}_n$ is a domain. The last inclusion is strict since $x\in(\mathfrak{p}_n,x)$ but $x\notin\mathfrak{p}_n[x]$ (the coefficient $1\notin\mathfrak{p}_n$). This yields a chain of length $n+1$, proving the inequality.

**Applying it.** From $\dim R[x]\le 1$ and $\dim R[x]\ge\dim R+1$ we get $\dim R\le 0$. Since $R$ is an integral domain, the only prime ideal in a $0$-dimensional domain is $(0)$, which is maximal, so $R/(0)=R$ is a field. Thus $R$ is a field.

**Consistency check.** If $R$ is a field, then $R[x]$ is a Euclidean domain (hence a PID), and in a PID every non-zero prime ideal is maximal. So the hypothesis is satisfied. ✓

**Why (b) and (c) are not forced.** The hypothesis forces $R$ to be a field, but a field need not contain $\mathbb{Z}$ (e.g. $R=\mathbb{F}_p(t)$ does not contain $\mathbb{Z}$) and need not contain any $\mathbb{F}_p$ (e.g. $R=\mathbb{Q}$ contains $\mathbb{Z}$ but no $\mathbb{F}_p$). So (b) and (c) are not consequences of the hypothesis.

Therefore the correct option is **(a)**.

